  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current Erds upper bound of 0.380868 is close to the target of 0.3808. The provided algorithm uses a sophisticated combination of gradient-based optimization, simulated annealing, and local search, but it may be stuck in a local optimum. The refined approach will:\n1. Use multi-resolution optimization, starting from coarser grids and interpolating to higher resolutions\n2. Employ an adaptive noise injection strategy during Descents\n3. Incorporate hybrid local-global searches at select grid sizes\n4. Use an efficient rolling window neighborhood search\n5. Replace direct function copying with interpolation-based transfers\n\nThe key innovations are:\n- Progressive resolution escalation by interpolation\n- Controlled stochastic disturbance to escape local optima\n- Baker's transform neighborhood testing for higher-dimensional searches\n- Just-in-time constraint restoration via local optimization\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe optimize function h(x): [0,2] -> [0,1] with:\n- E[1] is fixed to satisfy integral(h)=1\n- Variables are discretized to n points with dx=2/n\n- Objective: min(max_k integral h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n- Evaluation: np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')) * (2/n)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with parent interpolation (n_start)\n   - Generate random candidates at resolution n_low\n   - Keep budget tracker (deadline enforcement)\n\n2. Multi-resolution optimization loop:\n   - Sort grid sizes by descending merit\n   - Perform Budget-controlled Descent:\n        * Clean feasibility (project_feasible) \n        * Inject controlled stochastic noise (epsilon = 0.005 * (cell_size_factor))\n        * Gradient/informed neighborhood exploration \n        * Use Baker's transform for dimensionality escape\n   - Track best improvement criteria\n\n3. Hybris search states:\n   - Invert evaluations for unexpected stagnation\n   - Use rolling windows (radius=50) for efficient locality\n   - Combine SA with targeted interpolation transfers\n   - Set configurable decay schedule for budgets\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Primary grid: 1000 points (n_start)\n   - Secondary grids: [400,250,150,100]\n   - Moves:\n        \u2022 Neighborhood window: max-shift=20% resolution\n        \u2022 Stochastic: max change=1% (except impulsive updates)\n        \u2022 Interpolation transfers: max change=1.5%\n   - Scheduled by resolution decay:\n\n5. Deadline-aware handling:\n   - Safe early exit upon threshold crossing\n   - Progressive resolution fallback\n   - Moving deadline allocation by phase\n\n6. Validation:\n   - E[1] enforced via interpolation projection\n   - Objective reevaluated for top candidates\n   - Cardinality constraints (n) preserved via interpolation",
